            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;



The air leakage was tested to be 4.9 ACH (Air changes per hour) @ 50 Pa. This amount of air leakage isnÂt too bad but there is room for improvement.

The following areas were noted to have air leakage

-Window frames. On a few windows there was air getting in around the frame. This would take removing the trim and spray foaming the gap. Use spray foam meant for windows and doors since it expands less.

-Basement header. Spray foaming this space will greatly reduce air leakage in the basement. Since most wires and pipes exit the basement in this space as well, those gaps will be filled at the same time.

-The bathroom fans in both bathrooms had a little bit of air leakage. The damper on the vents should be checked to make sure they are closing correctly.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 120&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <ceilings>

Ceilings (Hip)



The attic of your house was assumed to have 8 inches (R28) of blown-in cellulose insulation. I have modelled adding 6 more inches (R20) of blown cellulose.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 6 inches (R20) blown cellulose insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 100&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Basement



Your basement walls are 8 inches of concrete with fiberglass batt insulation on most walls and 1.5 inches of extruded polystyrene on the rest. There are many ways to insulate the basement walls, but I have modelled if you were to remove the fiberglass and bring it up to 3 inches of extruded polystyrene to the entire wall. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 440&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;12.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 625 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Header



The header space in your basement has fiberglass batt insulation stuffed in to each joist space. The fiberglass is providing some insulation value but this area will have a lot of air leakage. The following savings calculation only takes into account the change in insulation and not the decrease in air leakage.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 5 inches of spray foam insulation to the basement header space, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 45&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>

COMBI LOOP



You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.



If you were to switch your furnace to a boiler, it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water via a dedicated loop.



Another option would be to replace your electric tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

If you were to switch your boiler to a condensing propane boiler it should have a similar savings to a condensing hot water tank and you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1085&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;





---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Drain Water Heat Recovery



A drain water heat recovery (DWHR) system is a way to reclaim some of the wasted heat that normally goes down the drain. The DWHR is located on a vertical section of the drain stack and prewarms water before it goes to the hot water heater. They come in various sizes and generally, the longer they are the more efficient they will be. A DWHR with an efficiency of at least 30% is required to qualify for a rebate through AEA.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a DWHR system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 160&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 700 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;





---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Insulation Blanket



Hot water tanks will lose heat into the surrounding room, and since electricity is more expensive than heating fuel, it is more important to keep the heat in the tank. Insulation blanket kits are available that can be easily installed by the homeowner.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an R5 insulation jacket on the hot water tank, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 110&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

OIL FURNACE



According to the serial number on the furnace, it is from 1987 and is estimated to have an efficiency of 80% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. Some furnaces may be available with direct vent which means the chimney is a pipe that goes out a side wall, which means the existing chimney can be sealed off. You should also look for sealed combustion, which means the combustion air for the furnace attaches directly to the furnace which prevents the furnace from using air from inside the house.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with an oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 510&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;15.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. 



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



PROPANE FURNACE



Another option for heating would be to switch to a propane-fired furnace, these tend to be more efficient than oil-fired. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1325&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;33.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



PROPANE BOILER



If you are considering switching your furnace to a boiler I recommend a high efficiency, condensing propane boiler. A propane boiler has the ability to provide hot water heating using a dedicated loop. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1305&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;32.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



COMBUSTION AIR



I did not see a combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the furnace. If you are considering switching your furnace out, you may be required to install a combustion air duct if the new heating system isnÂt sealed combustion. If a duct is required, it should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler or furnace is operating.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



The circulation pump for the domestic cold water can be switched out to an ECM pump. Since it is on a potable water line, the pump would need to have stainless steel flanges. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$ 175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 

